**BÁNH CUỐN** is one of my favourite Vietnamese dishes.

It is simple, soft, light, and full of flavour. At first glance, it may look like a simple rice roll. However, making good bánh cuốn requires skill and practice.

<!--more-->

### 1. What is BÁNH CUỐN?
Bánh cuốn is a Vietnamese steamed rice roll.

The main ingredient is a thin batter made from rice flour and water. The batter is spread into a very thin layer and steamed.

After steaming, the rice sheet is carefully removed. It can be served plain or filled with different ingredients.

A common version is bánh cuốn nhân thịt, which is filled with:
- minced pork;
- wood ear mushrooms;
- shallots or onions.

The rice roll is soft and slightly chewy. The filling adds a rich and savoury taste.

Bánh cuốn is usually served with:
- Vietnamese dipping sauce
- Vietnamese pork sausage (chả lụa)
- fried shallots
- fresh herbs
- cucumber
- bean sprouts

The combination is simple but delicious.

### 2. Origin of BÁNH CUỐN
Bánh cuốn has a long history in Vietnamese food culture.

It is especially associated with northern Vietnam. One of the best-known versions is bánh cuốn Thanh Trì, from Thanh Trì District in Hanoi.

Traditional bánh cuốn is made by steaming a very thin layer of rice batter.

The technique looks simple, but it takes practice.

The cook needs to:
- Prepare the rice batter.
- Heat the steaming cloth.
- Spread a thin layer of batter.
- Steam it for a short time.
- Remove the rice sheet carefully.
- Add the filling.
- Roll it gently.

The rice sheet needs to be thin but strong enough to roll without breaking.

Today, bánh cuốn can be found in many parts of Vietnam. Different regions and restaurants have developed their own versions.
### 3. Types of BÁNH CUỐN
There are several versions of bánh cuốn in Vietnam.
#### Bánh cuốn nhân thịt
This is one of the most common versions.

The rice sheet is filled with a mixture of:
- minced pork;
- wood ear mushrooms;
- onions or shallots.

The filling gives the soft rice roll a rich and savoury flavour.

#### Bánh cuốn trứng
includes an egg.

The egg can be cooked together with the rice sheet or served as part of the filling.

This version is especially popular in some northern Vietnamese restaurants.

#### Bánh cuốn with different local styles

Different regions may serve bánh cuốn with different ingredients.

For example, the type of sausage, herbs, vegetables, and dipping sauce can change from one place to another.

This is one of the interesting things about Vietnamese food.

The same dish can have different local styles.
### 4. Sauce for BÁNH CUỐN
The dipping sauce is an important part of bánh cuốn.

Vietnamese dipping sauce is commonly called nước chấm.

A simple version can include:
- fish sauce;
- water;
- sugar;
- lime juice or vinegar;
- garlic;
- chilli.

The balance between these ingredients is important.

It should be salty, sweet, sour, and slightly spicy.

The sauce also helps bring all the flavours together.

Soft rice rolls + savoury filling + fresh herbs + crispy fried shallots + nước chấm make a very balanced dish.

### Final Thoughts
Bánh cuốn may look simple, but there is a lot of skill behind it.

The rice sheet needs to be thin, soft, and smooth. The filling needs to be savoury. The dipping sauce needs to have a good balance of flavours.

For me, bánh cuốn is a good example of Vietnamese cooking.

It does not need many expensive ingredients.

Instead, it uses simple ingredients and careful cooking techniques to create a delicious meal.

If you visit Vietnam, I recommend trying bánh cuốn at a local restaurant or street food shop.

And if you have the chance to watch someone make bánh cuốn, take a close look.

The process of spreading and steaming the rice batter is just as interesting as eating it!

